#include <iostream>
#include <utility>

class Probe {
public:
    Probe() { std::cout << "ctor "; }
    Probe(const Probe&) { std::cout << "copy "; }
    Probe(Probe&&) noexcept { std::cout << "move "; }
    Probe& operator=(const Probe&) { std::cout << "copy= "; return *this; }
    Probe& operator=(Probe&&) noexcept { std::cout << "move= "; return *this; }
    ~Probe() { std::cout << "~ "; }
};

int main() {
    Probe a;
    Probe b = a;             // copy ctor or assignment?
    Probe c = std::move(a);  // move ctor or assignment?
    b = a;                   // which operator=?
    b = std::move(c);        // which operator=?
}
```

independentExercise.solution:
(a) The implicit copy constructor/assignment copy `f_` - two Tickets closing one FILE* in their destructors (double close, UB). The implicit moves are not generated at all (destructor declared), so a moved Ticket actually copies - aliasing again. (b) ```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <stdexcept>

class Ticket {
    std::FILE* f_;
public:
    explicit Ticket(const char* path) : f_(std::fopen(path, "r")) {
        if (!f_) throw std::runtime_error("open failed");
    }
    ~Ticket() { if (f_) std::fclose(f_); }
    Ticket(const Ticket&) = delete;                 // one owner
    Ticket& operator=(const Ticket&) = delete;
    Ticket(Ticket&& other) noexcept : f_(std::exchange(other.f_, nullptr)) {}  // steal, leave empty
    Ticket& operator=(Ticket&& other) noexcept {
        if (this != &other) {
            if (f_) std::fclose(f_);
            f_ = std::exchange(other.f_, nullptr);
        }
        return *this;
    }
};
``` Copies are deleted (ownership is exclusive), moves steal the handle and null the source - valid but empty. (c) ```cpp
struct Ticket {
    std::unique_ptr<std::FILE, decltype(&fclose)> f_;
    explicit Ticket(const char* path)
        : f_(std::fopen(path, "r"), &fclose) {
        if (!f_) throw std::runtime_error("open failed");
    }
};
``` Nothing special is declared: unique_ptr's destructor calls fclose, moves are automatically correct, copies are automatically deleted. Only the constructor policy (open-or-throw) remains hand-written.

